# Haryana Culture: Folk Dances, Saang, Fairs and Crafts

> Haryana's culture is rural, martial and musical: stamping men's dances,
> women's circular dances, all-night Saang plays and a calendar of
> fairs from Surajkund to Kurukshetra. In HSSC CET and Haryana Police
> papers this area gives quick marks, because the questions pair a name
> with one fact: who dances it, where a fair is held, or which craft
> belongs to which town. This lesson organises those pairs.

## 1. Folk Dances: Who, When, Where

The state's Art and Cultural Affairs department describes the chief
dances as follows.

| Dance | Performed by | Occasion or region |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Ghoomar | Girls and women | Festivals such as Holi, Gangaur and Teej; western Haryana, close to the Rajasthan border |
| Khoria | Women only | Harvest and farm-work occasions; central Haryana |
| Phag | Farmers, men and women | Month of Phalgun, near Holi |
| Dhamal | Men only (Ahir community) | Time of harvest; Gurgaon (Gurugram) region |
| Loor | Girls | Phalguna and Holi, linked with sowing of rabi crops; Bangar area; songs in question-answer form |
| Jhumar | Young married women | Accompanied by dholak and thali; also called "Haryanvi Gidda" |
| Daph | Farmers | Spring, in celebration of a good harvest; played on the one-sided drum called daph |
| Gugga | Men only | Procession in memory of Gugga Pir |
| Chaupaiya | Men and women | Devotional dance with manjira (hand cymbals) |
| Ras Leela | Braj-style performers | Songs praising Krishna; Braj area of Faridabad district |

[definition]
**Folk dances of Haryana** fall into three loose groups: **festive-seasonal**
(Phag, Teej and Sawan songs, Gugga), **devotional** (Chaupaiya, Ras Leela,
Manjira) and **ceremonial-recreational** (Dhamal, Ghoomar, Khoria, Loor).
[/definition]

Memory trick: **Dhamal = Ahir men of the Gurugram region**; **Khoria and
Loor = women/girls**. If the option says "only men" and "Gurgaon", it is
Dhamal. The word **Phag** comes from Phalgun, so link it with Holi.

## 2. Saang, Raagni and Music

**Saang** (also called Swang) is Haryanvi musical folk theatre, usually
staged in the open and mixing dialogue, songs and humour. **Raagni** is a
sung form and song category. Haryanvi saang and raagni reached a peak with
**Pandit Lakhmi Chand** (1903-1945) of Jaanti Kalan, Sonipat, honoured as
**Suryakavi** and often called the Kalidas of Haryana. His saangs include
Jyani Chor, Heer Ranjha and Bhagat Puranmal.

| Folk form | Fact |
| --- | --- |
| Saang | Folk theatre; Lakhmi Chand is the best-known name |
| Raagni | Sung narrative form, closely tied to saang |
| Alha-Khand | Heroic ballad cycle, associated with the 12th century warriors Alha and Udal |
| Teej and Holi songs | Women's seasonal songs |
| Sanjhi and Mangal songs | Devotional and ceremonial songs sung by women |

Another popular tale told in saang and song is **Puran Bhagat**.

## 3. Dialects of Haryana

Hindi is the official language of the state; Punjabi and English are
additional official languages. The spoken tongues vary region by region.

| Dialect | Area |
| --- | --- |
| Bangru (Haryanvi) | Central and eastern parts |
| Bagri | Western Haryana (Sirsa, Fatehabad, Hisar, Bhiwani areas, near Rajasthan) |
| Ahirwati | Southern Haryana (Ahirwal: Rewari, Mahendragarh, Gurugram) |
| Mewati | Southern and southeastern Mewat region |
| Braj | Southeast, near Uttar Pradesh |
| Punjabi and Hindi | Northeast (Panchkula, Ambala belt) |

The dialect map follows the dry-to-wet and west-to-east gradient: Bagri in
the west touches Rajasthani, Ahirwati and Mewati in the south, Braj near
Mathura and Punjabi in the north.

## 4. Fairs and Festivals

| Fair or festival | Place | When and what |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Surajkund International Crafts Mela | Surajkund, Faridabad | First fortnight of February; started 1987; theme state concept from 1989 (Rajasthan first); became international in 2013 |
| International Gita Mahotsav | Kurukshetra (Brahma Sarovar) | Around Gita Jayanti in the Margashirsha month (Nov-Dec) |
| Pinjore Heritage Festival | Yadavindra Gardens, Pinjore (Panchkula) | Annual festival, held in December, with folk dance and craft bazaars |
| Teej | Across Haryana | Sawan (monsoon) festival; swings and songs, Ghoomar performed |
| Gugga Navami | Villages across the state | Ninth day of the dark half of Bhadrapada; Gugga Pir is remembered with processions and a chhari |
| Holi and Phag | Villages | Phalgun; Phag, Loor and Daph dances |
| Baisakhi, Diwali Utsav | Statewide | Spring harvest festival and autumn festival of lights |

[example]
**Question:** The theme state concept at the Surajkund Mela was introduced in
1989. Which state was first? **Answer:** Rajasthan. Each year one state
presents its crafts, food and artistes; the Mela itself dates from 1987.
[/example]

## 5. Crafts, Dress and Society

**Crafts.** **Phulkari** ("flower work") is embroidery on handspun cotton,
common in Punjab and Haryana, and is made in many types such as Bawan
Bagh and Satranga. **Panipat** is the City of Weavers, known for durries
and carpets. **Khes** is a cotton double-cloth bed cover linked with Panipat
and nearby areas. Panipat is also known for woven **modhas** (round stools).

**Dress.** Men traditionally wear a **dhoti or kurta** with a **pagri**
(turban), and a chequered blanket in winter. Women wear a flared
**ghagra** (daaman), a short **angia** blouse and an **odhni** drawn over
the head.

**Khap panchayats.** A **khap** is a clan or village-group body, mostly of
Jat communities in Haryana and western Uttar Pradesh. A **Sarv Khap** is an
assembly of several khaps. Khaps have no official legal authority, and
their rulings on same-gotra marriages have been widely criticised; the
Supreme Court has condemned violence ordered by such bodies as illegal.

**Folk deities.** **Gugga Pir (Gogaji, Zahir Pir)** is a snake-protecting folk
deity whose main shrine is Gogamedi in Rajasthan. **Jathera** is the
worship of male ancestors of a village or clan, a distinctive Haryana
custom.

## 6. Exam Traps

- Surajkund is a mela (1987); Kurukshetra's festival is the Gita Mahotsav.
- Ghoomar belongs to the west; Dhamal to Gurugram's Ahirs; Gugga is a male procession.
- Lakhmi Chand is linked with saang and raagni, not Surajkund or Phulkari.
- Panipat is for durries, carpets and handlooms; Phulkari is the regional embroidery.

[summary]
Haryana's folk dances include Ghoomar (west), Khoria (women, central),
Phag (Phalgun), Dhamal (Ahir men, Gurugram), Loor (girls, Holi) and Daph
(spring harvest). Saang and raagni are folk theatre and song forms, with
Pandit Lakhmi Chand as their star. Dialects are Bangru, Bagri, Ahirwati,
Mewati and Braj. Major fairs are the Surajkund Crafts Mela (1987,
Faridabad), the International Gita Mahotsav (Kurukshetra) and the Pinjore
Heritage Festival (December). Panipat gives durries and handlooms, Phulkari
is the embroidery, and khaps and the folk deity Gugga Pir are social
markers.
[/summary]
